      'title': 'Navigating Life with Type 1 Diabetes',
      'content': 'Managing Type 1 Diabetes is a full-time job that you never get a vacation from. Counting carbs, adjusting insulin, monitoring blood sugar—it’s exhausting. When I was diagnosed at 22, I felt like my life was over. I thought I would never be able to eat a piece of cake again, travel spontaneously, or engage in intense physical activities without fear of a dangerous hypoglycemic episode.\n\nBut ten years later, I can confidently say that I am thriving. Technology has been an absolute game changer. Getting a continuous glucose monitor (CGM) and a closed-loop insulin pump gave me back so much freedom. I no longer have to prick my fingers ten times a day, and the pump adjusts my basal rates automatically while I sleep, preventing those terrifying overnight lows.\n\nI’m currently training for a half marathon, something I never thought possible when I was first diagnosed. It requires meticulous planning—carrying glucose gels, adjusting my pump settings for exercise, and staying hyper-aware of how my body feels—but crossing that finish line will be worth every ounce of extra effort.\n\nTo the newly diagnosed: it gets easier. The math becomes second nature. You will learn how your body reacts to different foods, stress levels, and exercise routines. Don’t let this condition stop you from living the life you want. You control the diabetes; it doesn’t control you.',
