      'title': 'Recovering from Burnout: Reclaiming My Life',
      'content': 'For years, I wore my 80-hour work weeks like a badge of honor. I thought I was invincible until my body physically forced me to stop. The burnout was so severe that I couldn\'t look at a computer screen without getting a migraine, and the exhaustion felt deep in my bones. I was running on empty, fueled entirely by caffeine, adrenaline, and a toxic fear of failure.\n\nTaking a medical leave of absence was the hardest but most necessary decision of my life. The first few weeks were terrifying. Without my job, I didn\'t know who I was. I felt incredibly guilty for resting. But it took months of aggressive rest, therapy, and completely unlearning my relationship with productivity to feel human again. I had to realize that my worth as a human being is not tied to my economic output or how many items I cross off a to-do list.\n\nI slowly began rediscovering the things that brought me actual joy, divorced from any sort of hustle or monetization. I started baking, reading fiction, and taking walks without listening to a productivity podcast. \n\nToday, I have strict boundaries. I clock out at 5 PM. I leave my phone in another room on weekends. I say no to projects that I don\'t have the capacity for. If you feel yourself slipping into burnout, please listen to your body before it breaks down entirely. Rest is a requirement, not a reward for hard work.',
