      'content': 'If you\'ve never had chronic insomnia, it\'s hard to explain the sheer panic of watching the clock tick from 2 AM to 3 AM to 4 AM, knowing you have to be at work in three hours. For over a year, I was functioning on 2-3 hours of broken sleep a night. It severely affected my job performance, my relationships, and my overall sanity. I felt like a zombie walking through a waking nightmare.\n\nSleeping pills provided temporary relief but weren\'t a long-term fix, and I hated the groggy feeling the next day. What finally worked for me was Cognitive Behavioral Therapy for Insomnia (CBT-I). It was arguably harder than the insomnia at first. I had to undergo strict sleep restriction—limiting my time in bed to only 5 hours a night initially—and completely rebuild my sleep hygiene from the ground up.\n\nI removed all screens from my bedroom, established a rigid wind-down routine consisting of reading a physical book and gentle stretching, and learned how to challenge the catastrophic thoughts about not sleeping that were keeping my nervous system in hyper-arousal.\n\nLast night, I slept for 7 unbroken hours. I woke up feeling rested and clear-headed—a sensation I had completely forgotten existed. There is a way out of the insomnia trap, it just requires immense discipline and a willingness to confront your anxiety head-on.',
