      'title': 'The Silent Struggle of Migraines',
      'content': 'People who have never experienced one often dismiss migraines as "just a bad headache." They don\'t understand the visual aura that blinds you, the debilitating nausea, the extreme sensitivity to light and sound that forces you into a dark room, and the cognitive "hangover" (the post-drome) that lasts for days after the pain subsides.\n\nFor years, I was getting 10-15 migraines a month. I felt like a burden to my family, constantly missing out on events, and I was incredibly unreliable at work. The fear of triggering an attack dominated every decision I made. Finding the right preventative medication was a grueling, years-long game of trial and error.\n\nAfter failing three different classes of older drugs—beta blockers, antidepressants, and anti-seizure meds—we finally tried a CGRP inhibitor injection. Within two months, it gave me my life back. It was nothing short of miraculous. \n\nI now get maybe one minor migraine a month, and it is usually easily manageable with an acute rescue medication. Don\'t settle for suffering. Keep pushing your neurologist for new treatments, because migraine science is advancing rapidly, and new options are becoming available every year.',
